d  compared to sarsa but it has lower  variance and therefore in some sense is  strictly preferable to sarsa  queue learning happens to be a special  case and we can just consider that by  considering pi to be the greedy policy  with respect to our action value  functions  and if that is the case then this  summation over policies will just  basically pick out the max and then we  can see this this full update becomes  exactly q learning so we can see that  this algorithm is actually a slightly  more general version of q learning  okay this brings us to the summary  of this lecture  now we're just going to briefly recap  basically the whole lecture and we're  going to start with a model-free policy  iteration  and recall that the point there was to  learn action values to predict the  current policy because that's how policy  evaluation works and policy evaluation  is a sub-component of policy iteration  and then we can do the other part which  is policy improvement for instance make  the policy footy greedy or epsilon  grading  and we talked about how we could extend  this idea of policy iteration to be  model free where both the evaluation  step and the improvement step can be  done model-free  then we talked about q learning and  noted that this is akin to value  iteration  where instead of having a separate phase  where we maybe evaluate a policy over  some time and then make it make it  greedy or improve it we could also do  this in a very tight loop where we  immediately make the policy greedy on  every step so when we update our action  values maybe the policy can change  immediately  and then q-learning is a model-free  algorithm that is very akin to this  value iteration idea  we've also talked about sarsa and just  now about expected sarsa and these can  be used more similarly to policy  iteration where we could also keep the  policy that we're evaluating fixed for a  while and on policy estimate that that  behavior and then do an improvement step  or we could also immediately update the  the policy as we go so sarsa could also  be used online with an epsilon greedy  policy where every time step you're just  re-evaluating what are the actions that  should be taken and we talked about the  convergence properties of these  algorithms as well  but sometimes we're also just interested  in maybe estimating a completely  different policy this in general is  called off policy learning  where expected sarsa could for instance  be used to pick any target policy that  we're interested in and then trying to  learn about the values of that learning  about the greedy policies then a special  case of policy learning which is the one  that is used by q learning  in general we want the behavior and the  target policy and policies to improve  that's what it means when we say that we  want to do control  and if the target policy is greedy  the behavior policy could still be  epsilon greedy or some other type of  behavior policy and then we could  construct this target for q-learning as  we saw earlier where we just take the  first reward as always this is a  temporal difference method and then we  bootstrap on the state value which is  defined by taking the greedy policy in  that state  written out here quite verbosely and  this is equivalent to just considering  taking the maximum action value in that  state  so conceptually what q loading is doing  is it's imagining that in the next state  it would pick greedy according to its  current estimates and then trying to use  that as an estimate for the value and  backing this up  in sarsa similarly but slightly  different leader target and behavioral  policies are the same and we're actually  considering the actual action that was  taken on the next time step before we  update our state action values at time  step t  then if we want this to converge because  it's an on policy algorithm we need the  additional requirement that the policy  becomes greedy so q learning is an off  policy cont